## Precedent P-19: Organizing the Harvest (United States, 1943 to 1944)

When the war strained the food system, the United States government asked amateurs, office workers, schoolchildren, grandmothers, to grow food. Not as a symbol. As logistics.

And it worked on a scale that's hard to believe now. By the National Park Service's account, in 1944 some 18.5 million Victory Gardeners supplied about forty percent of the nation's fresh vegetables. [National Park Service, Victory Gardens on the World War II Home Front](https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/victory-gardens-on-the-world-war-ii-home-front.htm) Not agribusiness. Yards, vacant lots, schoolyards, and rooftops, run by people the food industry would have written off as amateurs the year before.

It didn't happen by itself, either. The USDA's 1943 program told organizers to think about the bus route: a family without a yard needed a plot they could reach by streetcar, bus, or bicycle. Somebody had to get permission for the land, prepare it, assign plots, arrange water, and supply tools and advice, and the county agricultural agent was supposed to judge whether the ground was fit. School gardens needed paid help through the summer. A shortage of pressure canners prompted plans for shared canning centers, because a vegetable left to rot isn't food. [USDA, The 1943 Victory Garden Program](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-PR32_4400-4381b818c763ffa7d714174f6cc65de7/pdf/GOVPUB-PR32_4400-4381b818c763ffa7d714174f6cc65de7.pdf)

And there's a part the cheerful posters don't carry. Japanese Americans grew Victory Gardens inside the camps where their own government had imprisoned them. Their skill made life better under conditions they never chose. A national food campaign didn't mean equal standing in the nation.

Then the war ended, the campaign stopped, and the capacity faded, which is part of why your neighbor thinks food comes from a truck.

**The mechanism.** Distributed production scales sideways. Millions of small plots came online faster than any central capacity could have been built, because the capacity already existed, dormant, in ordinary soil and ordinary hands. The bottleneck was the decision to organize it: land, water, seed, advice, and a way to get the harvest to a kitchen.

**The rule.** Standby capacity is real, and so is its limit: a vegetable harvest is a contribution, not a complete diet.

**The practice.**

1. Activate one plot this season, however small: a raised bed, three buckets on a balcony, a community-garden plot. Then measure the output honestly: kilograms harvested, meals made. It'll be small the first year, and that's fine. The Victory Gardens weren't built on experts. They were built on millions of first-years.
2. Make it a network, not a hobby. One garden is produce; a street of gardens is infrastructure. Find the two or three people nearest you who grow anything, trade one variety or one skill this season, and follow one harvest all the way to someone's table.
3. Keep the standby system warm: save seed from one crop, keep the tools maintained and the beds alive over winter, and grow at least one thing every year. Capacity that never gets exercised is a rumor, not a capability.

