## The Foundations

At some point early this century, a lot of us fell for one of the best magic tricks ever performed. We were handed free email, free social media, and free video hosting, and in exchange we handed over our attention, our behavioral data, and a fair bit of our autonomy. We weren't the customers. We were the crop. Every photo, post, and video helped build the systems now learning to do our jobs.

But a magic trick only works while the audience looks where the magician points. Step behind the curtain and it falls apart.

## Why the machine needs you

Chapter 10 walked through model collapse: models trained on the output of earlier models lose their tails and drift toward mush. The flip side is this chapter's whole argument. In a world filling up with synthetic content, **something that actually happened, recorded by the person it happened to, is scarce.** Your stories, your mistakes, your local experiments, the record of what failed on the ridge and why: that's the valuable stuff now. Chapter 10's algorithmic judo is how you get it read. This chapter is about keeping control of it once it is.

## Let the useful thing travel

A small community can't do all the work of feeding humanity. It can figure out something useful and make it easier for the next community to learn. A repair that took a week might take the next person an afternoon because somebody left an honest record. A failed trial can keep the next group from spending scarce money on the same mistake.

That's the hopeful answer to scale. People and places differ, but the knowledge they trade doesn't have to stay locked inside the group that earned it. Share the conditions along with the result, so someone else can judge what carries over. The controller records are useful precisely because they say what was tested and what still failed. And don't mistake Denny's audience for a forecast of income; it's a fictional success. Most useful sharing is quieter: a phone call between two kitchens, a corrected pickup notice, a drawing another repairer can follow.

## People before the story

Ask permission before publishing someone's image, circumstances, address, or request for help. Consent to receive food isn't consent to become content. Give people a route to participate without appearing in the documentation.

Removing file metadata can reduce one kind of exposure. It doesn't remove a street sign in the photograph or stop a caption from identifying someone. Review the actual exported material, including what is visible and what your words reveal.

A local copy, a content address, and encryption do different jobs. Availability depends on someone keeping and serving the data. A content identifier doesn't promise perpetual hosting. Physical transfer avoids some network exposure but doesn't make a file immune to loss, damage, or unauthorized copying.

## Media autonomy: keep your own press

Platforms are a temporary distribution channel, not a home. If everything you've made lives on servers owned by someone else, it can vanish with a policy change or a closed account.

**Clean your files.** Photos from a phone usually carry metadata embedded in the file, EXIF data, which can include the GPS coordinates where the picture was taken, the date and time, and the device. Free tools like exiftool or Metadata Cleaner can strip it. That handles one kind of exposure. It doesn't remove a street sign in the background or a caption that names someone, so look at what you're actually publishing.

**Distribute copies, not just links.** Peer-to-peer systems like IPFS address a file by what's in it, a cryptographic fingerprint, rather than by which server it's on, and anyone can help host a copy. That's great for keeping something findable. It isn't a promise of permanence: a file stays available only as long as someone keeps it and serves it.

**Keep a sneakernet.** Sometimes the most reliable network is a person with a pocket. A single small memory card can hold a whole library of manuals, maps, and a full offline copy of Wikipedia. Passing drives hand to hand avoids a lot of network exposure. It doesn't make a file immune to loss, damage, or copying, so encrypt what's private and keep more than one copy.

**Build a local archive.** Kiwix will give you all of English Wikipedia offline. Add the manuals, repair guides, and records your community actually uses, on a drive or two that someone keeps current. You're now the librarian of your neighborhood.

![The Sneakernet Cycle: a hyper-local node encrypts data onto a microSD card, which is passed by hand or local courier to a neighbor node that decrypts it, and the cycle repeats in reverse](/book-images/ch16-sneakernet.svg)

*The sneakernet cycle. A library walks across town in a pocket.*

## Creative immunity

Making things isn't only logistics. It's how you keep your mind.

The feed is built to keep you reacting. When you write, draw, compose, code, or build, you flip from receiver to transmitter, and your attention comes back to you. A community that stops telling its own stories eventually starts believing the ones told about it. Keep your history, your humor, your failures, and your hope in your own words.

## Who gets to speak

A service can collect excellent operating data and still tell a self-congratulatory story. The organizer reports meals distributed; someone receiving them reports that pickup happens during their shift. Both statements can be true. If only the first reaches the public account, the project has made its own reputation easier to protect than its recipients.

Give people a way to correct the story without risking assistance. Publish changes prompted by criticism. Tell readers which improvements have reached the people served and which remain plans. The author of this book owes you the same discipline: enthusiasm is a reason to look harder at whether a promise holds.

We can want the machines to help and still report a machine's failure plainly. An ambition that can survive correction is more useful than an image that must never crack.

## This week's handoff

1. **Clean your output.** Install a metadata stripper and make cleaning photos a habit before anything leaves your devices.
2. **Start the archive.** Download Wikipedia with Kiwix and one shelf's worth of manuals your household or co-op actually needs. Put a copy on a second drive.
3. **Make one useful thing for someone else.** Offer a food group a simple choice: a corrected public schedule, a checked translation, a clear page on how to ask for help, or a short account of one repair. Let them choose, including "none of those." Finish it, and confirm who'll keep it current.

