## Precedent P-20: An Argument in Circulation (Philadelphia and London, 1776)

Thomas Paine had been in America barely a year. He was a staymaker's son and a twice-dismissed excise officer: no office, no fortune, no name worth borrowing. What he had was a printer and a way of writing that ordinary people could read aloud in a tavern.

*Common Sense* came off Robert Bell's press in Philadelphia in January 1776, anonymous, in a paper wrapper, for two shillings. [Clements Library, Going Viral in 1776](https://clements.umich.edu/exhibit/revolutionary-paine/going-viral-in-1776/) It ran through printing after printing that year and helped turn a quarrel about taxes into an argument for independence, in language people could carry into their own kitchens.

But look at what actually happened on paper, because it's the whole lesson of media power in miniature. Paine and his printer fell out almost immediately. Paine meant his share of the profits to buy winter clothing for the Continental army; Bell claimed there were no profits. So Paine took an enlarged edition to William and Thomas Bradford. [University of Minnesota, Common Sense](https://librarycollections.law.umn.edu/treasures/index.php?npage=13) Across the Atlantic, some London editions cut the passages about the king, and in one surviving copy of John Almon's edition, a reader filled the gaps back in by hand. The author framed the argument. Printers could reproduce it, revise it, or censor it. Readers could fight back in the margins.

**The mechanism.** Leverage is the ratio of reach to permission. Paine needed nobody's approval beyond one printer. When a new medium drops the cost of reach, the first people to use it gain influence far out of proportion to their credentials. But whoever controls the press still has a hand on the message.

**The rule.** Give people something clear enough to discuss and check, and own enough of your own press that nobody else gets the last edit.

**The practice.**

1. Write your pamphlet, even if it's four pages. Pick the one thing you know from experience that would really help the person two years behind you, and write it the way Denny talked on camera: plain speech, what happened, what you'd do differently. Publish it this month.
2. Own your printing press before you need it. A platform account is a stall in someone else's market; an email list and a site you control are a press. Set both up now, even with an audience of nine, and point every platform reader toward them. When the algorithm changes, the people on your list are still yours.
3. Run the tavern test. Read your latest piece out loud. Every sentence you stumble on, every word you'd never say to a neighbor over a fence, rewrite in plain speech. Then ask one reader to explain it back to you and name its weakest point, and fix that before you share it wider.

